<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">The independence polynomial of a graph encapsulates all independent sets of differing sizes, a task classified as NP-hard in theoretical computer science. This article examines the independence polynomial of zero divisor graphs in commutative rings. We demonstrate that the independent sets, represented as a sequence of coefficients of the independence polynomial, exhibit unimodality and log-concavity. Therefore, for the independence polynomial of some zero divisor graphs, the unimodal conjecture is true. Additionally, the characteristics of the zeros of the independence polynomial are delineated, along with their corresponding annular regions on the plane.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> Zero divisor graphs, Independence polynomial; NP-hard problem; Zeros; Unimodal; Log-concave</p>
</div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2020 AMS subject classification:</span> 05C25, 05C31, 68R05, 05C69, 26C10, 68Q25.

<span id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">ACM classification: F.2.2</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All graph sconsidered in this article are finite, simple, connected and undirected. A graph is denoted as <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G=G(V(G),E(G))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G=G(V(G),E(G))</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the vertex set, and <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the edge set. The cardinal number <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|E(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|E(G)|</annotation></semantics></math> is the size <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|V(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|V(G)|</annotation></semantics></math> is the order <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>. The number of edges incident on a vertex <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{i}\in V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{i}\in V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">degree</span> of <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and is denoted as <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d_{v_{i}}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d_{v_{i}}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">union</span> of two graphs <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with disjoint vertex sets <math id="S1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and disjoint edge sets <math id="S1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{1}\cup G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{1}\cup G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, is defined as a graph with vertex set <math id="S1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}\cup V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}\cup V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and edge set <math id="S1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{1}\cup E_{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{1}\cup E_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The join of two graphs <math id="S1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, denoted by <math id="S1.p1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{1}\vee G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{1}\vee G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, is a graph with vertex set <math id="S1.p1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}\cup V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}\cup V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and edge set <math id="S1.p1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle E_{1}\cup E_{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle E_{1}\cup E_{2},</annotation></semantics></math> together with edges <math id="S1.p1.m26" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\big\{\{u,v\}:u\in V(G_{1}),v\in V(G_{2})\big\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\big\{\{u,v\}:u\in V(G_{1}),v\in V(G_{2})\big\}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A polynomial <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}\ell_{i}x^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}\ell_{i}x^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">unimodal</span>, if the coefficient sequence <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\Big\{\ell_{i}\Big\}_{i=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\Big\{\ell_{i}\Big\}_{i=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> form a unimodal sequence, that is, we can find a positive integer <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p~(0\leq p\leq n)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0.330em">⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p~(0\leq p\leq n),</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{0}\leq\ell_{1}\leq\dots\leq\ell_{p}\geq\ell_{p+1}\geq\dots\geq\ell_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{0}\leq\ell_{1}\leq\dots\leq\ell_{p}\geq\ell_{p+1}\geq\dots\geq\ell_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, the coefficients of <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> increase to a certain value, and thereafter decreases. The polynomial <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is said to be <em id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">log-concave</em> if</p>
