<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{j}^{2}\geq\ell_{j-1}\ell_{j+1},\quad\text{for all}\quad 1\leq j\leq n-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for all</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{j}^{2}\geq\ell_{j-1}\ell_{j+1},\quad\text{for all}\quad 1\leq j\leq n-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1.1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A positive log-concave sequence is known to be unimodal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. However, if <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{i}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{i}=0</annotation></semantics></math> implies that either <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{1}=\dots=\ell_{i-1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{1}=\dots=\ell_{i-1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{i+1}=\dots=\ell_{n}=0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">⋯</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{i+1}=\dots=\ell_{n}=0,</annotation></semantics></math> (no internal zeros), then a non-negative log-concave sequence is unimodal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. In topological graph theory, log-concavity of sequences is connected with surface embedding of graphs, comparable to log-concavity of genus polynomials of graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. The polynomial <math id="S1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+2x+3x^{2}+4x^{3}+3x^{4}+2x^{5}+x^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+2x+3x^{2}+4x^{3}+3x^{4}+2x^{5}+x^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> is unimodal, while <math id="S1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 12+7x+9x^{2}+16x^{3}+4x^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 12+7x+9x^{2}+16x^{3}+4x^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is not. A log-concave polynomial is <math id="S1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p(x)=x^{5}+5x^{4}+10x^{3}+10x^{2}+5x+1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p(x)=x^{5}+5x^{4}+10x^{3}+10x^{2}+5x+1,</annotation></semantics></math> while <math id="S1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q(x)=x^{5}+10x^{4}+5x^{3}+5x^{2}+10x+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q(x)=x^{5}+10x^{4}+5x^{3}+5x^{2}+10x+1</annotation></semantics></math> is not.
Many polynomials related to graph invariants like matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, chromatic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and reliability polynomials <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>, are thought to exhibit unimodal coefficient (absolute value) sequences under different base expansions. However, there are still conjectures open related to independence polynomial of graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
These polynomial characteristics are mostly investigated for graph polynomials, such as the independent domination polynomial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>, dominating polynomial, matching polynomial, clique polynomial, and several others <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R</annotation></semantics></math> is a commutative ring <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, a non-zero element <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x\in R</annotation></semantics></math> is a zero divisor of <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R</annotation></semantics></math> if there is a non-zero <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y\in R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y\in R</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x\cdot y=0." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x\cdot y=0.</annotation></semantics></math> Beck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> introduced the concept of zero divisor graphs to study the coloring of rings. Beck defined zero divisor graph <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, with vertices as zero divisors including identity <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and connected two distinct vertices if and only if their product is zero. So, it follows that <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to all vertices of <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(R)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Anderson and Livingston <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>, omitting <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(R)</annotation></semantics></math>, connected two non-zero zero divisors if and only if their product is zero.
A vast literature is available on zero divisor graphs and their applications. Anderson and Weber <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> characterized all zero divisor graphs with at most <math id="S1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 14</annotation></semantics></math> vertices. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>, the authors established the domination polynomial of the zero divisor graph of rings. Gürsoy et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite> introduced the idea of independent domination polynomials for zero divisor graphs in <math id="S1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for specific values of <math id="S1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n.</annotation></semantics></math> The theory of independent domination polynomial theory is recently developed in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. The zero divisor graphs are universal graphs, that is, for an integer modulo ring <math id="S1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> (or even in boolean ring), given any finite graph of order <math id="S1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is some <math id="S1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> is an induced subgraph of zero divisor graph <math id="S1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Some interesting properties of zero divisor graphs were studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. We will revise basic properties of zero divisor graphs of commutative ring <math id="S1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We will discuss the structure of <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rho_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rho_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> be the proper divisor of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math>, then we consider the sets</p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{\rho_{i}}=\{x\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}:\gcd(x,n)=\rho_{i}\},\quad\text{for}\quad 1\leq i\leq k," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>for</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{\rho_{i}}=\{x\in\mathbb{Z}_{n}:\gcd(x,n)=\rho_{i}\},\quad\text{for}\quad 1\leq i\leq k,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k</annotation></semantics></math> is some positive integer other than <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n.</annotation></semantics></math> Also, note that <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gcd(x,n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gcd(x,n)</annotation></semantics></math> is the greatest common divisor of <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n.</annotation></semantics></math>
Also, <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}\cap V_{\rho_{j}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}\cap V_{\rho_{j}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle i\neq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle i\neq j</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the vertex set of <math id="S1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned as <math id="S1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\rho_{1}},V_{\rho_{2}},\dots,V_{\rho_{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\rho_{1}},V_{\rho_{2}},\dots,V_{\rho_{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, in graph <math id="S1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, the vertices of <math id="S1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent to vertices of <math id="S1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\rho_{j}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\rho_{j}}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rho_{i}\rho_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rho_{i}\rho_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiple of <math id="S1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n.</annotation></semantics></math> The cardinality of <math id="S1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varphi\left(\tfrac{n}{\rho_{i}}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varphi\left(\tfrac{n}{\rho_{i}}\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S1.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varphi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varphi</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euler’s Totient function. In addition, the induced subgraph of <math id="S1.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{\rho_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> is either a clique or its complement.
Furthermore, the induced subgraph of <math id="S1.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(V_{\rho_{i}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(V_{\rho_{i}})</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S1.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{\varphi\left(\frac{n}{\rho_{i}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{\varphi\left(\frac{n}{\rho_{i}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\rho_{i}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\rho_{i}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S1.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n,</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise it is <math id="S1.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{K}_{\varphi\left(\frac{n}{\rho_{i}}\right)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{K}_{\varphi\left(\frac{n}{\rho_{i}}\right)}</annotation></semantics></math>. A complete graph is denoted by <math id="S1.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{n},</annotation></semantics></math> and its complement by <math id="S1.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{K}_{n}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{K}_{n}.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">The article is organized as: Section <a href="#S2" title="2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> gives the independence polynomial of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n\in\{p,p^{2},p^{3},pq\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n\in\{p,p^{2},p^{3},pq\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> are primes. Also, we find their unimodal, log-concave properties and discuss their zeros. In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of ℤ_{𝑝^2⁢𝑞} ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we give the independence polynomial of <math id="S1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})</annotation></semantics></math>, investigate its zero, log-concave and unimodal properties. In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of order 𝑝⁢𝑞⁢𝑟 ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we present the independence polynomial of <math id="S1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math> are primes. We give bounds for its zeros and illustrate with examples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independent set</em> in a graph <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of vertices no two of which are adjacent. For a graph <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G,</annotation></semantics></math> the cardinality of the maximum size of an independent set, is called the <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independence number</em> of <math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, and is denoted by <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. The associated <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">decision problem</em></p>
<table id="S2.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{Independent set}=\{(G,k):\alpha(G)\geq k\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Independent set</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo rspace="0.278em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{Independent set}=\{(G,k):\alpha(G)\geq k\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is one of the classical NP-complete problems, and the optimization problem of computing <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is therefore NP-hard, where <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k</annotation></semantics></math> is a positive integer. Karp showed in his seminal list of NP-complete problems that the independent set problem is NP-complete, and a fundamental problem <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. Since, the independent set problem is equivalent (under complementation) to clique set in <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, known inapproximability bounds for clique number transfer immediately. It is NP-hard to approximate the maximum independent set within a factor of <math id="S2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n^{1-\varepsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n^{1-\varepsilon}</annotation></semantics></math> for any fixed <math id="S2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, unless <math id="S2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>NP</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. The NP-completeness of independent set implies that, unless <math id="S2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>NP</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{P}=\mathrm{NP}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is no polynomial-time algorithm that computes <math id="S2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha(G)</annotation></semantics></math> on general graphs. Moreover, the strong inapproximability results rule out polynomial-time algorithms with approximation ratio <math id="S2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n^{1-\varepsilon},\varepsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n^{1-\varepsilon},\varepsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, placing the problem among the most intractable maximization problems from the perspective of worst-case approximation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s_{k}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s_{k}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of independent sets of size <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the <em id="S2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independence polynomial</em> of <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> is the generating function of possible independent sets, and is given as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G,x)\;=\;\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha(G)}s_{k}(G)\,x^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.391em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G,x)\;=\;\sum_{k=0}^{\alpha(G)}s_{k}(G)\,x^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The polynomial <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(G,x)</annotation></semantics></math> was introduced and studied systematically in the 1980s and has since become a standard enumerative invariant with connections to statistical physics (the hard–core model), combinatorial optimization, and algebraic graph theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Evaluating <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(G,x)</annotation></semantics></math> at special points recovers several natural quantities. In particular, <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(G,1)=\sum_{k\geq 0}s_{k}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(G,1)=\sum_{k\geq 0}s_{k}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> counts all independent sets of <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(G,0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(G,0)=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I^{\prime}(G,0)=s_{1}(G)=|V(G)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I^{\prime}(G,0)=s_{1}(G)=|V(G)|</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(G,x)</annotation></semantics></math> is (up to a trivial change of variable) the partition function of the hardcore lattice gas on <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> with activity <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math>; this perspective underlies many structural and analytic results <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>.
If <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math> are vertex disjoint graphs, then</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G\cup H,x)\;=\;I(G,x)\,I(H,x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G\cup H,x)\;=\;I(G,x)\,I(H,x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G\cup H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G\cup H</annotation></semantics></math> is the union of graphs. The above expression follows as an independent set in <math id="S2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G\cup H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>∪</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G\cup H</annotation></semantics></math> is precisely the disjoint union of an independent set of <math id="S2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> and one of <math id="S2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Let <math id="S2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle L(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle L(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the line graph of <math id="S2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m_{k}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m_{k}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> be the number of <math id="S2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k</annotation></semantics></math>-matchings of <math id="S2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>, then we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k\geq 0}m_{k}(G)\,x^{k}\;=\;I\bigl(L(G),x\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mrow><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k\geq 0}m_{k}(G)\,x^{k}\;=\;I\bigl(L(G),x\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">Thus, many properties of independence polynomials transfer to matching generating functions, and vice versa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>.
The coefficient sequence <math id="S2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\bigl(s_{0}(G),s_{1}(G),\dots,s_{\alpha(G)}(G)\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\bigl(s_{0}(G),s_{1}(G),\dots,s_{\alpha(G)}(G)\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly positive and obeys basic combinatorial inequalities. In particular, when <math id="S2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(G,x)</annotation></semantics></math> has only real zeros, then the Newton’s inequalities imply that the coefficient sequence is log-concave, and hence unimodal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. If <math id="S2.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> is claw-free (that is, contains no induced star <math id="S2.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{1,3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{1,3}</annotation></semantics></math>), then <math id="S2.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(G,x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(G,x)</annotation></semantics></math> has only real zeros <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Consequently its coefficients are log-concave and unimodal. This result is sharp, in general graphs, independence polynomials need not be real-rooted. Active topics include root location for broader graph classes, stability and zero-free regions motivated by statistical physics, extremal problems for the coefficient sequences, and algorithmic approximation on sparse graph families. Surveys such as <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> provide a comprehensive entry point and extensive bibliographies. A graph of order <math id="S2.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math> is very well-covered if every maximal independent set has size <math id="S2.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{n}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{n}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, like the regular complete bipartite graphs <math id="S2.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{m,m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{m,m}</annotation></semantics></math> is very well-covered.
A graph is well-covered graphs, if all its maximal independent sets have the same size (complete graphs and the cycle are examples).
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, the authors conjectured that the independence coefficients of well-covered graphs were unimodal, and showed that every graph
can be embedded as an induced subgraph of such a well-covered graph. However, Michael and Traves <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> later disproved the conjecture. A conjecture due to Alavi et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> that is still open is that the independence polynomial of a tree is unimodal. Levit and Mandrescu <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> conjectured that the independence polynomial of every very well-covered graphs are unimodal, and to date, the conjecture remains open. Though there are progress for certain families of graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>.
The independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G_{1}\vee G_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G_{1}\vee G_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> ((see, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>) is</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G_{1}\vee G_{2},x)=I(G_{1},x)+I(G_{2},x)-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G_{1}\vee G_{2},x)=I(G_{1},x)+I(G_{2},x)-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p2.5" class="ltx_p">With this information and the fact that the independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{K}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{K}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1+x)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1+x)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and that of <math id="S2.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1+nx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1+nx</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we discuss the independence polynomial of zero divisor graphs of ring <math id="S2.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for certain values of <math id="S2.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n.</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p</annotation></semantics></math>, the ring is <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a field, since <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math> is prime. A field has no zero divisors other than 0. The set of vertices of the zero divisor graph <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p})</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of non-zero zero divisors, which is an empty graph.
The independence polynomial is <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p}),x)=i_{0}x^{0}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p}),x)=i_{0}x^{0}=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of non-zero zero divisors in <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the elements <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\in\{1,\dots,p^{2}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\in\{1,\dots,p^{2}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gcd(a,p^{2})&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gcd(a,p^{2})&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. This is equivalent to <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\mid a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\mid a</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∣</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mid</annotation></semantics></math> means divide. The vertex set is <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V=\{kp\mid 1\leq k\leq p-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V=\{kp\mid 1\leq k\leq p-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|V|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|V|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{1}=k_{1}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{1}=k_{1}p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{2}=k_{2}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{2}=k_{2}p</annotation></semantics></math> be two distinct vertices in <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq k_{1},k_{2}\leq p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq k_{1},k_{2}\leq p-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{1}\neq k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{1}\neq k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their product is <math id="S2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v_{1}v_{2}=(k_{1}p)(k_{2}p)=k_{1}k_{2}p^{2}\equiv 0\pmod{p^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v_{1}v_{2}=(k_{1}p)(k_{2}p)=k_{1}k_{2}p^{2}\equiv 0\pmod{p^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, every pair of distinct vertices is adjacent. The graph <math id="S2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete graph <math id="S2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}})</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}}),x)=i_{0}x^{0}+i_{1}x^{1}=1+(p-1)x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}}),x)=i_{0}x^{0}+i_{1}x^{1}=1+(p-1)x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math> be a prime and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})</annotation></semantics></math> be zero divisor graph of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the independence polynomial of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(1+(p-1)x)-1=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(p-1)x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(1+(p-1)x)-1=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(p-1)x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
