<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
For <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{3}</annotation></semantics></math>, the set of non-zero zero divisors in <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math> are the multiples of <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{1,\dots,p^{3}-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{1,\dots,p^{3}-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We partition the vertex set <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V</annotation></semantics></math> into two disjoint subsets: <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}=\{a\in V\mid\gcd(a,p^{3})=p\}=\{kp\mid 1\leq k&lt;p^{2},p\nmid k\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>gcd</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}=\{a\in V\mid\gcd(a,p^{3})=p\}=\{kp\mid 1\leq k&lt;p^{2},p\nmid k\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{2}=\{a\in V\mid\gcd(a,p^{3})=p^{2}\}=\{kp^{2}\mid 1\leq k&lt;p\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{2}=\{a\in V\mid\gcd(a,p^{3})=p^{2}\}=\{kp^{2}\mid 1\leq k&lt;p\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\nmid" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\nmid</annotation></semantics></math> represents does not divide. The sizes of these sets are: <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|V_{1}|=(p^{2}-1)-(p-1)=p^{2}-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|V_{1}|=(p^{2}-1)-(p-1)=p^{2}-p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|V_{2}|=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|V_{2}|=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a=k_{1}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a=k_{1}p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b=k_{2}p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b=k_{2}p</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct vertices in <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\nmid k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\nmid k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\nmid k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\nmid k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then their product is <math id="S2.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ab=k_{1}k_{2}p^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ab=k_{1}k_{2}p^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p6.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, it does not divide <math id="S2.p6.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p6.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p6.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\nmid k_{1}k_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\nmid k_{1}k_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.p6.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ab\not\equiv 0\pmod{p^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>≢</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ab\not\equiv 0\pmod{p^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and it follows that there are no edges between any two vertices in <math id="S2.p6.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. The subgraph induced by <math id="S2.p6.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is an edgeless graph <math id="S2.p6.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{K}_{p^{2}-p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{K}_{p^{2}-p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p6.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a=k_{1}p^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a=k_{1}p^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b=k_{2}p^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b=k_{2}p^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct vertices in <math id="S2.p6.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then their product is <math id="S2.p6.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ab=k_{1}k_{2}p^{4}=(k_{1}k_{2}p)p^{3}\equiv 0\pmod{p^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ab=k_{1}k_{2}p^{4}=(k_{1}k_{2}p)p^{3}\equiv 0\pmod{p^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, every pair of distinct vertices in <math id="S2.p6.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent. So, the subgraph induced by <math id="S2.p6.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete graph <math id="S2.p6.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p6.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a=k_{1}p\in V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a=k_{1}p\in V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b=k_{2}p^{2}\in V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b=k_{2}p^{2}\in V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Their product is <math id="S2.p6.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ab=(k_{1}p)(k_{2}p^{2})=k_{1}k_{2}p^{3}\equiv 0\pmod{p^{3}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ab=(k_{1}p)(k_{2}p^{2})=k_{1}k_{2}p^{3}\equiv 0\pmod{p^{3}}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, if follows that every vertex in <math id="S2.p6.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex in <math id="S2.p6.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.p6.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})\cong K_{|V_{2}|}\vee\overline{K}_{|V_{1}|}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})\cong K_{|V_{2}|}\vee\overline{K}_{|V_{1}|}.</annotation></semantics></math> Keeping in mind the independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p6.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{K}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{K}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p6.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(1+(p-1)x)-1=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(p-1)x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(1+(p-1)x)-1=(1+x)^{p^{2}-p}+(p-1)x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.7pt;height:5.7pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The following result gives unimodal and log-concave property of <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The independence polynomial <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> of the zero divisor graph <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}})</annotation></semantics></math>, with prime <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave and unimodal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S2.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{3}}),x)</annotation></semantics></math>, and with <math id="S2.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{2}-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{2}-p</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x)=(1+x)^{n}+(p-1)x=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}x^{k}+(p-1)x." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x)=(1+x)^{n}+(p-1)x=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\binom{n}{k}x^{k}+(p-1)x.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p8.2" class="ltx_p">Let the coefficient sequence of <math id="S2.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="S2.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\ell_{k})_{k=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\ell_{k})_{k=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have
<math id="S2.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{0}=\binom{n}{0}=1,\ell_{1}=\binom{n}{1}+p-1=(p^{2}-p)+p-1=p^{2}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>0</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>1</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{0}=\binom{n}{0}=1,\ell_{1}=\binom{n}{1}+p-1=(p^{2}-p)+p-1=p^{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k\in\{2,3,\dots,n\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k\in\{2,3,\dots,n\}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Clearly, the coefficients of <math id="S2.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are positive for <math id="S2.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we must have <math id="S2.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{1}^{2}\geq\ell_{0}\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{1}^{2}\geq\ell_{0}\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Which translates to <math id="S2.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(p^{2}-1)^{2}\geq 1\cdot\binom{p^{2}-p}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(p^{2}-1)^{2}\geq 1\cdot\binom{p^{2}-p}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and gives <math id="S2.p8.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(p-1)^{2}(p+1)^{2}\geq\tfrac{p(p-1)(p^{2}-p-1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(p-1)^{2}(p+1)^{2}\geq\tfrac{p(p-1)(p^{2}-p-1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. It simplifies to <math id="S2.p8.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2(p-1)(p+1)^{2}\geq p(p^{2}-p-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2(p-1)(p+1)^{2}\geq p(p^{2}-p-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is true for <math id="S2.p8.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\geq 2." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\geq 2.</annotation></semantics></math> For <math id="S2.p8.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p8.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{2}^{2}\geq\ell_{1}\ell_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{2}^{2}\geq\ell_{1}\ell_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> implies that <math id="S2.p8.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{p^{2}-p}{2}^{2}\geq(p^{2}-1)\binom{p^{2}-p}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{p^{2}-p}{2}^{2}\geq(p^{2}-1)\binom{p^{2}-p}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For <math id="S2.p8.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=2</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequality <math id="S2.p8.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{2}^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{2}^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> is trivially true. For <math id="S2.p8.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S2.p8.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{2}-p\geq 6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{2}-p\geq 6</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequality is
<math id="S2.p8.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left(\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\right)^{2}\geq(p^{2}-1)\tfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>6</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left(\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\right)^{2}\geq(p^{2}-1)\tfrac{n(n-1)(n-2)}{6}</annotation></semantics></math>. And by dividing <math id="S2.p8.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, it yields <math id="S2.p8.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\geq(p^{2}-1)\tfrac{n-2}{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{n(n-1)}{2}\geq(p^{2}-1)\tfrac{n-2}{3}</annotation></semantics></math>.
<math id="S2.p8.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 3n(n-1)\geq 2(p^{2}-1)(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 3n(n-1)\geq 2(p^{2}-1)(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math>, which after simplification gives <math id="S2.p8.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(p-1)^{3}+5\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(p-1)^{3}+5\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the inequality holds. For <math id="S2.p8.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider <math id="S2.p8.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{j}=\binom{n}{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{j}=\binom{n}{j}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p8.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j\in\{k-1,k,k+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j\in\{k-1,k,k+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, we must show <math id="S2.p8.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{n}{k}^{2}\geq\binom{n}{k-1}\binom{n}{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{n}{k}^{2}\geq\binom{n}{k-1}\binom{n}{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a standard property of binomial coefficients. It is equivalent to the ratio test <math id="S2.p8.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{\binom{n}{k}}{\binom{n}{k-1}}\geq\tfrac{\binom{n}{k+1}}{\binom{n}{k}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{\binom{n}{k}}{\binom{n}{k-1}}\geq\tfrac{\binom{n}{k+1}}{\binom{n}{k}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="S2.p8.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{n-k+1}{k}\geq\tfrac{n-k}{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{n-k+1}{k}\geq\tfrac{n-k}{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. This simplifies to <math id="S2.p8.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(n-k+1)(k+1)\geq k(n-k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(n-k+1)(k+1)\geq k(n-k)</annotation></semantics></math>, which reduces to <math id="S2.p8.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n+1\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n+1\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. That is true as <math id="S2.p8.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{2}-p\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{2}-p\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the coefficient sequence <math id="S2.p8.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\ell_{k})_{k=0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\ell_{k})_{k=0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p8.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave, and a log-concave positive real sequence is unimodal.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.7pt;height:5.7pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we discuss the independent polynomial of the zero divisor graph of <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> with primes <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=pq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=pq</annotation></semantics></math> with primes <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R=\mathbb{Z}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R=\mathbb{Z}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the vertices of the graph <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(R)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(R)</annotation></semantics></math> are the non-zero zero divisors of <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R</annotation></semantics></math>. An element <math id="S2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\in\{1,\dots,pq-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\in\{1,\dots,pq-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> is a zero divisor if and only if <math id="S2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gcd(a,pq)\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gcd(a,pq)\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, which is equivalent to <math id="S2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p|a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p|a</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q|a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q|a</annotation></semantics></math>. The vertex set of <math id="S2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> is partitioned into two sets: <math id="S2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}=\{kp\mid 1\leq k\leq q-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}=\{kp\mid 1\leq k\leq q-1\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}=\{lq\mid 1\leq l\leq p-1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}=\{lq\mid 1\leq l\leq p-1\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The zero divisor <math id="S2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> is the complete bipartite graph <math id="S2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle K_{q-1,p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle K_{q-1,p-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with parts <math id="S2.p10.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.p10.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x,y\in V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x,y\in V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> be distinct vertices. Then <math id="S2.p10.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x=ap" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x=ap</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=bp," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=bp,</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p10.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq a,b\leq q-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq a,b\leq q-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Their product <math id="S2.p10.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle xy=abp^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle xy=abp^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is zero in <math id="S2.p10.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S2.p10.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle pq|abp^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle pq|abp^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="S2.p10.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q|abp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q|abp</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S2.p10.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gcd(p,q)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gcd(p,q)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, this requires <math id="S2.p10.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q|ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q|ab</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p10.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q</annotation></semantics></math> is prime, <math id="S2.p10.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q|a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q|a</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.p10.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q|b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q|b</annotation></semantics></math>, which is impossible as <math id="S2.p10.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq a,b\leq q-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq a,b\leq q-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, no two vertices in <math id="S2.p10.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent. By a symmetric argument, no two vertices in <math id="S2.p10.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent. Let <math id="S2.p10.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x\in V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x\in V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y\in V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y\in V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p10.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x=ap" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x=ap</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=bq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=bq</annotation></semantics></math> for some integers <math id="S2.p10.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a,b</annotation></semantics></math>. Their product is <math id="S2.p10.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle xy=(ap)(bq)=ab(pq)\equiv 0\pmod{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≡</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle xy=(ap)(bq)=ab(pq)\equiv 0\pmod{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, every vertex in <math id="S2.p10.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex in <math id="S2.p10.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
So, it follows that <math id="S2.p10.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> is a complete bipartite graph with partitions <math id="S2.p10.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p10.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Applying (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), and noting that <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})\cong K_{q-1,p-1}\cong\overline{K}_{p-1}\vee\overline{K}_{q-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>≅</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∨</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})\cong K_{q-1,p-1}\cong\overline{K}_{p-1}\vee\overline{K}_{q-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> as given below</p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)=(1+x)^{q-1}+(1+x)^{p-1}-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)=(1+x)^{q-1}+(1+x)^{p-1}-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2.3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">The following result gives the details about the real zeros of <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math> be a zero of the polynomial <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The properties of the zeros depend on the primes <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p,q</annotation></semantics></math> are odd primes. Then there are exactly two real zeros, one in the interval <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and another in <math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(-2,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(-2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q</annotation></semantics></math> is an odd prime, there are exactly two real zeros, one in the interval <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and another in <math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(-3,-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(-3,-2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)=(1+x)^{q-1}+(1+x)^{p-1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)=(1+x)^{q-1}+(1+x)^{p-1}-1</annotation></semantics></math> be an independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=1+x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=1+x</annotation></semantics></math>, the problem reduces to finding the roots of the equation <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y^{q-1}+y^{p-1}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y^{q-1}+y^{p-1}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. We analyze the roots based on the parity of the primes <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q</annotation></semantics></math> are odd primes, then <math id="S2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p-1=2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p-1=2k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q-1=2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q-1=2m</annotation></semantics></math> for some integers <math id="S2.p13.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k,m\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k,m\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, the equation becomes <math id="S2.p13.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y^{2m}+y^{2k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y^{2m}+y^{2k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. For real <math id="S2.p13.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S2.p13.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z=y^{2}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z=y^{2}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the equation becomes <math id="S2.p13.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z^{m}+z^{k}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z^{m}+z^{k}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p13.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(z)=z^{m}+z^{k}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(z)=z^{m}+z^{k}-1</annotation></semantics></math>, then for <math id="S2.p13.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="S2.p13.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{\prime}(z)=mz^{m-1}+kz^{k-1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>z</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{\prime}(z)=mz^{m-1}+kz^{k-1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. So, the function <math id="S2.p13.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(z)</annotation></semantics></math> is strictly increasing. Clearly, <math id="S2.p13.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(0)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(0)=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there is a unique positive real root <math id="S2.p13.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{0}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{0}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. This gives two real roots for <math id="S2.p13.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y</annotation></semantics></math>, namely <math id="S2.p13.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=\pm\sqrt{z_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=\pm\sqrt{z_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p13.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0&lt;z_{0}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0&lt;z_{0}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p13.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0&lt;\sqrt{z_{0}}&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msqrt><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0&lt;\sqrt{z_{0}}&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. The corresponding zeros of <math id="S2.p13.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.p13.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{1}=\sqrt{z_{0}}-1\in(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msqrt><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msqrt><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{1}=\sqrt{z_{0}}-1\in(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p13.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{2}=-\sqrt{z_{0}}-1\in(-2,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><msqrt><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msqrt></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{2}=-\sqrt{z_{0}}-1\in(-2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. That proves (1).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=2</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q</annotation></semantics></math> is an odd prime, then the equation for <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y=1+x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y=1+x</annotation></semantics></math> becomes <math id="S2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y^{q-1}+y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y^{q-1}+y=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
With <math id="S2.p14.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(y)=y^{q-1}+y-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(y)=y^{q-1}+y-1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S2.p14.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{\prime}(y)=(q-1)y^{q-2}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{\prime}(y)=(q-1)y^{q-2}+1</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S2.p14.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q</annotation></semantics></math> is an odd prime, <math id="S2.p14.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q-2</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, and <math id="S2.p14.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f^{\prime}(y)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f^{\prime}(y)=0</annotation></semantics></math> has a unique real solution <math id="S2.p14.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{c}=-(1/(q-1))^{1/(q-2)}\in(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{c}=-(1/(q-1))^{1/(q-2)}\in(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a local minimum. Also,
<math id="S2.p14.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(0)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(0)=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p14.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(1)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(1)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so by Intermediate Value Theorem, there is a root <math id="S2.p14.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{1}\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{1}\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S2.p14.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y\to\pm\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>±</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y\to\pm\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S2.p14.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(y)\to+\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(y)\to+\infty</annotation></semantics></math>. The minimum value is <math id="S2.p14.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(y_{c})=y_{c}(1-1/(q-1))-1&lt;-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(y_{c})=y_{c}(1-1/(q-1))-1&lt;-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, there must be another real root <math id="S2.p14.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle y_{2}&lt;y_{c}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle y_{2}&lt;y_{c}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S2.p14.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(-1)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(-1)=-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p14.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle f(-2)=(-2)^{q-1}-3=2^{q-1}-3\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle f(-2)=(-2)^{q-1}-3=2^{q-1}-3\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p14.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, this second root lies in <math id="S2.p14.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(-2,-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(-2,-1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
The corresponding zeros of <math id="S2.p14.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are <math id="S2.p14.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{1}=y_{1}-1\in(-1,0)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{1}=y_{1}-1\in(-1,0)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p14.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{2}=y_{2}-1\in(-3,-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{2}=y_{2}-1\in(-3,-2)</annotation></semantics></math>. That proves part (2).
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.7pt;height:5.7pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p15.1" class="ltx_p">For the remaining zeros of <math id="S2.p15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x),</annotation></semantics></math> we have the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math> be a zero of the polynomial <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)</annotation></semantics></math>. With <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, the zeros <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the open annular region <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{1}&lt;|x|&lt;r_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{1}&lt;|x|&lt;r_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, where</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{1}=\frac{R-1}{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}\quad\text{and}\quad r_{2}=R+1,\quad\text{with}\quad R=2^{1/(q-p)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">with</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{1}=\frac{R-1}{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}\quad\text{and}\quad r_{2}=R+1,\quad\text{with}\quad R=2^{1/(q-p)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p16" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p16.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p16.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
By Equation (<a href="#S2.E3" title="In 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>), the independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S2.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)=(1+x)^{q-1}+(1+x)^{p-1}-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)=(1+x)^{q-1}+(1+x)^{p-1}-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p16.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z</annotation></semantics></math> be a zero of the polynomial <math id="S2.p16.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, such that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(z)=(1+z)^{q-1}+(1+z)^{p-1}-1=0." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(z)=(1+z)^{q-1}+(1+z)^{p-1}-1=0.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.3" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p16.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w=1+z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w=1+z</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.p16.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z=w-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z=w-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p16.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w</annotation></semantics></math> must be a zero of the new polynomial <math id="S2.p16.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)=w^{q-1}+w^{p-1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)=w^{q-1}+w^{p-1}-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The degree of <math id="S2.p16.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S2.p16.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q-1</annotation></semantics></math>, so there are <math id="S2.p16.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q-1</annotation></semantics></math> such zeros. Note that <math id="S2.p16.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(0)=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(0)=1</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.p16.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z=0</annotation></semantics></math> is not a zero, and consequently <math id="S2.p16.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w=1</annotation></semantics></math> is not a zero of <math id="S2.p16.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)</annotation></semantics></math>. We first establish bounds for the modulus of the zeros of <math id="S2.p16.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)</annotation></semantics></math>. We claim that any zero <math id="S2.p16.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p16.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)=w^{q-1}+w^{p-1}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)=w^{q-1}+w^{p-1}-1</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="S2.p16.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|&lt;2^{1/(q-p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|&lt;2^{1/(q-p)}</annotation></semantics></math>. The equation <math id="S2.p16.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)=0</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as <math id="S2.p16.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w^{q-1}=1-w^{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w^{q-1}=1-w^{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.p16.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|\leq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|\leq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the claim holds, since <math id="S2.p16.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p,q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p,q</annotation></semantics></math> are primes with <math id="S2.p16.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.p16.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q-p\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q-p\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="S2.p16.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2^{1/(q-p)}&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2^{1/(q-p)}&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Now, if <math id="S2.p16.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, then with the help of the triangle inequality, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|w|^{q-1}=|1-w^{p-1}|\leq 1+|w|^{p-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|w|^{q-1}=|1-w^{p-1}|\leq 1+|w|^{p-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.4" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p16.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x=|w|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x=|w|</annotation></semantics></math>, then with <math id="S2.p16.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p16.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p-1&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p-1&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, we can divide by <math id="S2.p16.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x^{p-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x^{p-1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and obtain</p>
<table id="S2.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{q-p}\leq\frac{1}{x^{p-1}}+1&lt;1+1=2." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{q-p}\leq\frac{1}{x^{p-1}}+1&lt;1+1=2.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.5" class="ltx_p">The above inequality is strict as <math id="S2.p16.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S2.p16.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|^{q-p}&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|^{q-p}&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies that <math id="S2.p16.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|&lt;2^{1/(q-p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|&lt;2^{1/(q-p)}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, with <math id="S2.p16.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R=2^{1/(q-p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R=2^{1/(q-p)}</annotation></semantics></math>, it shows that all zeros <math id="S2.p16.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p16.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)</annotation></semantics></math> lie inside the circle <math id="S2.p16.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|=R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|=R</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, we find the annular region for the zeros <math id="S2.p16.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z=w-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z=w-1</annotation></semantics></math>. The modulus of a zero <math id="S2.p16.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z</annotation></semantics></math> is given by <math id="S2.p16.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|z|=|w-1|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|z|=|w-1|</annotation></semantics></math>. By the triangle inequality and by <math id="S2.p16.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|^{q-p}&lt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|^{q-p}&lt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|z|=|w-1|\leq|w|+1&lt;R+1=2^{1/(q-p)}+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|z|=|w-1|\leq|w|+1&lt;R+1=2^{1/(q-p)}+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.6" class="ltx_p">Next, as <math id="S2.p16.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w</annotation></semantics></math> is a zero of <math id="S2.p16.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.p16.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Also <math id="S2.p16.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle w\neq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle w\neq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p16.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(1)=1^{q-1}+1^{p-1}-1=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(1)=1^{q-1}+1^{p-1}-1=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Now consider the difference <math id="S2.p16.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Q(w)-Q(1)=(w^{q-1}+w^{p-1}-1)-1=-1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Q(w)-Q(1)=(w^{q-1}+w^{p-1}-1)-1=-1.</annotation></semantics></math>
Also, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q(w)-Q(1)=(w^{q-1}-1)+(w^{p-1}-1)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>w</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q(w)-Q(1)=(w^{q-1}-1)+(w^{p-1}-1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.7" class="ltx_p">Comparing and factoring the differences of powers, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(w-1)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}w^{k}\right)+(w-1)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}w^{k}\right)=-1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(w-1)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}w^{k}\right)+(w-1)\left(\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}w^{k}\right)=-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.8" class="ltx_p">With <math id="S2.p16.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z=w-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z=w-1</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p16.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S(w)=\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}w^{k}+\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}w^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S(w)=\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}w^{k}+\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}w^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we get <math id="S2.p16.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z\cdot S(w)=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z\cdot S(w)=-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since <math id="S2.p16.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.p16.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S(w)\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S(w)\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and we can write <math id="S2.p16.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|z|=\tfrac{1}{|S(w)|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|z|=\tfrac{1}{|S(w)|}</annotation></semantics></math>.
To find a lower bound for <math id="S2.p16.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|z|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|z|</annotation></semantics></math>, we need an upper bound for <math id="S2.p16.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|S(w)|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|S(w)|</annotation></semantics></math>. So, by triangle inequality, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S(w)|\leq\left|\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}w^{k}\right|+\left|\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}w^{k}\right|\leq\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}|w|^{k}+\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}|w|^{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo>|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S(w)|\leq\left|\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}w^{k}\right|+\left|\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}w^{k}\right|\leq\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}|w|^{k}+\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}|w|^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.9" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.p16.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|w|&lt;R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|w|&lt;R</annotation></semantics></math>, the above geometric series sums are bounded as</p>
<table id="S2.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|S(w)|&lt;\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}R^{k}+\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}R^{k}=\frac{R^{q-1}-1}{R-1}+\frac{R^{p-1}-1}{R-1}=\frac{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}{R-1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munderover><msup><mi>R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|S(w)|&lt;\sum_{k=0}^{q-2}R^{k}+\sum_{k=0}^{p-2}R^{k}=\frac{R^{q-1}-1}{R-1}+\frac{R^{p-1}-1}{R-1}=\frac{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}{R-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.10" class="ltx_p">Therefore, we obtain</p>
<table id="S2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|z|=\frac{1}{|S(w)|}&gt;\frac{R-1}{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|z|=\frac{1}{|S(w)|}&gt;\frac{R-1}{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.11" class="ltx_p">Now, combining these results, the zeros <math id="S2.p16.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p16.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are located in the annular region</p>
<table id="S2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{R-1}{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}&lt;|z|&lt;R+1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{R-1}{R^{q-1}+R^{p-1}-2}&lt;|z|&lt;R+1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p16.12" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p16.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R=2^{1/(q-p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R=2^{1/(q-p)}</annotation></semantics></math>.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.7pt;height:5.7pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p17.1" class="ltx_p">We will illustrate Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 2.3. ‣ 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> by the examples. For <math id="S2.p17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=7</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p17.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q=17," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>17</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q=17,</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex22"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{119}),x)=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>119</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{119}),x)=</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex22.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle 1+22x+135x^{2}+580x^{3}+1835x^{4}+4374x^{5}+8009x^{6}+11440x^{7}+12870x^{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>22</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>135</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>580</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1835</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4374</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8009</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11440</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>7</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>12870</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle 1+22x+135x^{2}+580x^{3}+1835x^{4}+4374x^{5}+8009x^{6}+11440x^{7}+12870x^{8}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex23"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle+11440x^{9}+8008x^{10}+4368x^{11}+1820x^{12}+560x^{13}+120x^{14}+16x^{15}+x^{16}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>11440</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>9</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>8008</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>10</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4368</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>11</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>1820</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>12</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>560</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>13</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>120</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>14</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>15</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>16</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle+11440x^{9}+8008x^{10}+4368x^{11}+1820x^{12}+560x^{13}+120x^{14}+16x^{15}+x^{16}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p17.2" class="ltx_p">The zeros of the polynomial <math id="S2.p17.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{119}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>119</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{119}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> are shown in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a). Also, <math id="S2.p17.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle R=2^{(1/(q-p))}=1.12246," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1.12246</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle R=2^{(1/(q-p))}=1.12246,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p17.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{R-1}{R^{p-1}+R^{q-1}-2}=0.0306" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>R</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.0306</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{R-1}{R^{p-1}+R^{q-1}-2}=0.0306</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, by Theorem <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 2.4. ‣ 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a>, the annular region is <math id="S2.p17.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0.0306&lt;|z|&lt;2.12246" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.0306</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2.12246</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0.0306&lt;|z|&lt;2.12246</annotation></semantics></math> and the zeros of <math id="S2.p17.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{119}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>119</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{119}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> lie inside it, as can be seen in Figure <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S2.p18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=2," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=2,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p18.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q=13," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q=13,</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
