<div id="S2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Next result show that the independence polynomial <math id="S2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> of graph <math id="S2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math>
is unimodal and log-concave.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.5</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The polynomial <math id="S2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> is log-concave and unimodal.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.p20" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p20.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p20.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Since the independence polynomial of <math id="S2.p20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Gamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq})</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S2.p20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)=(1+x)^{n}+(1+x)^{m}-1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)=(1+x)^{n}+(1+x)^{m}-1,</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S2.p20.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=q-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=q-1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p20.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m=p-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m=p-1</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S2.p20.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math> are primes, so we have <math id="S2.p20.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq m&lt;n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq m&lt;n</annotation></semantics></math>. Let
<math id="S2.p20.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\ell_{k}x^{k}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n}\ell_{k}x^{k}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Then
<math id="S2.p20.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{0}=1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{0}=1,</annotation></semantics></math>
and, for <math id="S2.p20.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq k\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq k\leq n</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S2.p20.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}+\binom{m}{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}+\binom{m}{k},</annotation></semantics></math>
where we use the convention <math id="S2.p20.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{m}{k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{m}{k}=0</annotation></semantics></math> whenever <math id="S2.p20.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k&gt;m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k&gt;m</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus,
<math id="S2.p20.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}+\binom{m}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}+\binom{m}{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p20.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq k\leq m," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq k\leq m,</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S2.p20.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p20.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m+1\leq k\leq n." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m+1\leq k\leq n.</annotation></semantics></math>
We must show that
<math id="S2.p20.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k}^{2}\geq\ell_{k-1}\ell_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k}^{2}\geq\ell_{k-1}\ell_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.p20.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq k\leq n-1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq k\leq n-1.</annotation></semantics></math>
For <math id="S2.p20.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we have
<math id="S2.p20.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{1}^{2}-\ell_{0}\ell_{2}=\tfrac{n^{2}+m^{2}+4mn+n+m}{2}&gt;0," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{1}^{2}-\ell_{0}\ell_{2}=\tfrac{n^{2}+m^{2}+4mn+n+m}{2}&gt;0,</annotation></semantics></math> and hence the log-concavity inequality holds for <math id="S2.p20.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Next, assume that <math id="S2.p20.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\leq k\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\leq k\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>. If
<math id="S2.p20.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N_{j}=\binom{n}{j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N_{j}=\binom{n}{j},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p20.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{j}=\binom{m}{j}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>j</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{j}=\binom{m}{j},</annotation></semantics></math>
then
<math id="S2.p20.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{j}=N_{j}+M_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{j}=N_{j}+M_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.p20.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j=k-1,k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j=k-1,k</annotation></semantics></math>, while the same formula also holds for <math id="S2.p20.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle j=k+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle j=k+1</annotation></semantics></math>, if we agree that <math id="S2.p20.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle M_{m+1}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle M_{m+1}=0</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S2.p20.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=m</annotation></semantics></math>. We will prove that</p>
<table id="S2.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N_{k}+M_{k})^{2}\geq(N_{k-1}+M_{k-1})(N_{k+1}+M_{k+1})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N_{k}+M_{k})^{2}\geq(N_{k-1}+M_{k-1})(N_{k+1}+M_{k+1}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.2" class="ltx_p">or equivalently, by expanding, it is enough to prove that
<math id="S2.p20.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle N_{k}^{2}\geq N_{k-1}N_{k+1},M_{k}^{2}\geq M_{k-1}M_{k+1}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle N_{k}^{2}\geq N_{k-1}N_{k+1},M_{k}^{2}\geq M_{k-1}M_{k+1},</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S2.p20.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2N_{k}M_{k}\geq N_{k-1}M_{k+1}+M_{k-1}N_{k+1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2N_{k}M_{k}\geq N_{k-1}M_{k+1}+M_{k-1}N_{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math>
The first two inequalities are the standard log-concavity inequalities for binomial coefficients. It remains to prove the mixed type third inequality. As
<math id="S2.p20.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{r}{k}=\frac{r-k+1}{k}\binom{r}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{r}{k}=\frac{r-k+1}{k}\binom{r}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S2.p20.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{r}{k+1}=\frac{r-k}{k+1}\binom{r}{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>r</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{r}{k+1}=\frac{r-k}{k+1}\binom{r}{k},</annotation></semantics></math>
the mixed inequality becomes</p>
<table id="S2.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\binom{n}{k}\binom{m}{k}\geq\binom{n}{k-1}\binom{m}{k+1}+\binom{m}{k-1}\binom{n}{k+1}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\binom{n}{k}\binom{m}{k}\geq\binom{n}{k-1}\binom{m}{k+1}+\binom{m}{k-1}\binom{n}{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.3" class="ltx_p">Thus, by substituting their values, we have</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex28"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle 2\frac{n-k+1}{k}\binom{n}{k-1}\frac{m-k+1}{k}\binom{m}{k-1}\geq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle 2\frac{n-k+1}{k}\binom{n}{k-1}\frac{m-k+1}{k}\binom{m}{k-1}\geq</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex28.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle\binom{n}{k-1}\frac{m-k}{k+1}\frac{m-k+1}{k}\binom{m}{k-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle\binom{n}{k-1}\frac{m-k}{k+1}\frac{m-k+1}{k}\binom{m}{k-1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.Ex29"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle+\binom{m}{k-1}\frac{n-k}{k+1}\frac{n-k+1}{k}\binom{n}{k-1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>m</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle+\binom{m}{k-1}\frac{n-k}{k+1}\frac{n-k+1}{k}\binom{n}{k-1}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.4" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S2.p20.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\leq k\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\leq k\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>, the factors involved are non-negative, and after simplifying,
we obtain</p>
<table id="S2.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(k+1)(n-k+1)(m-k+1)\geq k(m-k)(m-k+1)+k(n-k)(n-k+1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(k+1)(n-k+1)(m-k+1)\geq k(m-k)(m-k+1)+k(n-k)(n-k+1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.5" class="ltx_p">With compact notations
<math id="S2.p20.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A=n-k+1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A=n-k+1,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p20.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle B=m-k+1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle B=m-k+1.</annotation></semantics></math>, we have</p>
<table id="S2.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(k+1)AB\geq k(B-1)B+k(A-1)A" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(k+1)AB\geq k(B-1)B+k(A-1)A</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.6" class="ltx_p">which is equivalent to</p>
<table id="S2.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(k+1)\geq k\left(1-\frac{1}{B}\right)+k\left(1-\frac{1}{A}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>B</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>A</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(k+1)\geq k\left(1-\frac{1}{B}\right)+k\left(1-\frac{1}{A}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.7" class="ltx_p">The right-hand side of above inequality is at most <math id="S2.p20.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2k</annotation></semantics></math>, while the left-hand side is <math id="S2.p20.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2k+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2k+2</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence the inequality holds, and <math id="S2.p20.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k}^{2}\geq\ell_{k-1}\ell_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k}^{2}\geq\ell_{k-1}\ell_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
for every <math id="S2.p20.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 2\leq k\leq m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 2\leq k\leq m</annotation></semantics></math>. For the boundary index <math id="S2.p20.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=m+1</annotation></semantics></math>, which occurs only when <math id="S2.p20.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle m+1\leq n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle m+1\leq n-1</annotation></semantics></math>, that is, when <math id="S2.p20.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n\geq m+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n\geq m+2</annotation></semantics></math>. So, <math id="S2.p20.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{m+1}^{2}\geq\ell_{m}\ell_{m+2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{m+1}^{2}\geq\ell_{m}\ell_{m+2}</annotation></semantics></math> gives</p>
<table id="S2.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\binom{n}{m+1}^{2}\geq\left(\binom{n}{m}+1\right)\binom{n}{m+2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\binom{n}{m+1}^{2}\geq\left(\binom{n}{m}+1\right)\binom{n}{m+2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.8" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p20.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a=n-m," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a=n-m,</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S2.p20.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S2.p20.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n\geq m+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n\geq m+2</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, with
<math id="S2.p20.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{n}{m+1}=\binom{n}{m}\frac{a}{m+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{n}{m+1}=\binom{n}{m}\frac{a}{m+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and
<math id="S2.p20.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{n}{m+2}=\binom{n}{m}\frac{a(a-1)}{(m+1)(m+2)}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{n}{m+2}=\binom{n}{m}\frac{a(a-1)}{(m+1)(m+2)},</annotation></semantics></math>
the inequality is equivalent to
<math id="S2.p20.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{n}{m}(n+1)\geq(a-1)(m+1)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{n}{m}(n+1)\geq(a-1)(m+1).</annotation></semantics></math>
Now <math id="S2.p20.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 1\leq m\leq n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 1\leq m\leq n-2</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S2.p20.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\binom{n}{m}\geq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\binom{n}{m}\geq n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p20.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(a-1)+(m+1)=n," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(a-1)+(m+1)=n,</annotation></semantics></math>
and hence</p>
<table id="S2.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a-1)(m+1)\leq\frac{n^{2}}{4}\leq n(n+1)\leq\binom{n}{m}(n+1)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a-1)(m+1)\leq\frac{n^{2}}{4}\leq n(n+1)\leq\binom{n}{m}(n+1).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p20.9" class="ltx_p">Therefore, the log-concavity inequality also holds at <math id="S2.p20.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k=m+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k=m+1</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, suppose <math id="S2.p20.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle k\geq m+2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle k\geq m+2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then
<math id="S2.p20.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k-1}=\binom{n}{k-1},\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k-1}=\binom{n}{k-1},\ell_{k}=\binom{n}{k},</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p20.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k+1}=\binom{n}{k+1}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k+1}=\binom{n}{k+1}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Thus,
<math id="S2.p20.m58" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\ell_{k}^{2}\geq\ell_{k-1}\ell_{k+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\ell_{k}^{2}\geq\ell_{k-1}\ell_{k+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
follows directly from the log-concavity of the binomial coefficients in the expansion of <math id="S2.p20.m59" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(1+x)^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(1+x)^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Combining all cases, the coefficient sequence
<math id="S2.p20.m60" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(\ell_{0},\ell_{1},\ldots,\ell_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(\ell_{0},\ell_{1},\ldots,\ell_{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
is log-concave. Since all coefficients are positive, the sequence has no internal zeros. A positive log-concave sequence is unimodal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>. Therefore, <math id="S2.p20.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(x)=I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pq}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> is both log-concave and unimodal.
 <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:5.7pt;height:5.7pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of <math id="S3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q}</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following result gives the independent polynomial of the zero divisor graph <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For primes <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q</annotation></semantics></math>, the independence polynomial of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q});x)=(1+x)^{(p-1)(p+q-1)}+(1+x)^{p(q-1)}-(1+x)^{(p-1)(q-1)}+(p-1)x(1+x)^{p(p-1)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q});x)=(1+x)^{(p-1)(p+q-1)}+(1+x)^{p(q-1)}-(1+x)^{(p-1)(q-1)}+(p-1)x(1+x)^{p(p-1)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G\cong\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G\cong\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q})</annotation></semantics></math> be the zero divisor graph of ring <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{Z}_{p^{2}q}.</annotation></semantics></math> The vertex set <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned into four disjoint sets based on the greatest common divisor (gcd) with <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=p^{2}q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=p^{2}q</annotation></semantics></math>, that is,
<math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{d}=\{k\in\{1,\dots,n-1\}\mid\gcd(k,n)=d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{d}=\{k\in\{1,\dots,n-1\}\mid\gcd(k,n)=d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The non-zero zero divisors correspond to <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\in\{p,p^{2},q,pq\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\in\{p,p^{2},q,pq\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and their cardinalities are:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex36"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle|V_{p}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle|V_{p}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex36.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\varphi(n/p)=\varphi(pq)=(p-1)(q-1),|V_{p^{2}}|=\varphi(n/p^{2})=\varphi(q)=q-1," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\varphi(n/p)=\varphi(pq)=(p-1)(q-1),|V_{p^{2}}|=\varphi(n/p^{2})=\varphi(q)=q-1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex37"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle|V_{q}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle|V_{q}|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex37.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\displaystyle=\varphi(n/q)=\varphi(p^{2})=p(p-1),|V_{pq}|=\varphi(n/pq)=\varphi(p)=p-1." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\displaystyle=\varphi(n/q)=\varphi(p^{2})=p(p-1),|V_{pq}|=\varphi(n/pq)=\varphi(p)=p-1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">For each <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\in\{p,q,p^{2}\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\in\{p,q,p^{2}\},</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is an independent set as <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is not multiple of <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, the <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is a clique, since <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(pq)^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(pq)^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is multiple of <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, each vertex of <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex of <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and each vertex of <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex of <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math>
Let <math id="S3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all independent sets of <math id="S3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math>. We partition <math id="S3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> into two disjoint collections:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{1}=\{S\in\mathcal{I}\mid S\cap V_{pq}=\emptyset\}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{I}_{2}=\{S\in\mathcal{I}\mid S\cap V_{pq}\neq\emptyset\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{1}=\{S\in\mathcal{I}\mid S\cap V_{pq}=\emptyset\}\quad\text{and}\quad\mathcal{I}_{2}=\{S\in\mathcal{I}\mid S\cap V_{pq}\neq\emptyset\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Based on the above decomposition, the independence polynomial of <math id="S3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> can be written as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G,x)=\sum_{S\in\mathcal{I}_{1}}x^{|S|}+\sum_{S\in\mathcal{I}_{2}}x^{|S|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></munder><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G,x)=\sum_{S\in\mathcal{I}_{1}}x^{|S|}+\sum_{S\in\mathcal{I}_{2}}x^{|S|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">First consider the set <math id="S3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, the independent sets of <math id="S3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G</annotation></semantics></math> are the independent sets of the subgraph <math id="S3.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G^{\prime}=\varGamma[V_{p}\cup V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G^{\prime}=\varGamma[V_{p}\cup V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}]</annotation></semantics></math>. As there are no edges between <math id="S3.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is the disjoint union of <math id="S3.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}]</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall that the independence polynomial of a disjoint union of graphs is the product of their independence polynomials.
So, the independence polynomial of <math id="S3.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p}]\cong\overline{K}_{|V_{p}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p}]\cong\overline{K}_{|V_{p}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma[V_{p}];x)=(1+x)^{|V_{p}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma[V_{p}];x)=(1+x)^{|V_{p}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. By (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of integral modulo ring ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), the independence polynomial of
<math id="S3.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}]\cong K_{|V_{p^{2}}|,|V_{q}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma[V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}]\cong K_{|V_{p^{2}}|,|V_{q}|}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S3.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma[V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}];x)=(1+x)^{|V_{p^{2}}|}+(1+x)^{|V_{q}|}-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma[V_{p^{2}}\cup V_{q}];x)=(1+x)^{|V_{p^{2}}|}+(1+x)^{|V_{q}|}-1</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, the total contribution from <math id="S3.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is</p>
<table id="S3.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I(G^{\prime},x)=(1+x)^{|V_{p}|}\left((1+x)^{|V_{p^{2}}|}+(1+x)^{|V_{q}|}-1\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I(G^{\prime},x)=(1+x)^{|V_{p}|}\left((1+x)^{|V_{p^{2}}|}+(1+x)^{|V_{q}|}-1\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.5" class="ltx_p">Next, consider <math id="S3.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\in\mathcal{I}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\in\mathcal{I}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. As <math id="S3.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> is a clique, and it follows that <math id="S3.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S</annotation></semantics></math> can contain at most one vertex from <math id="S3.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. So, <math id="S3.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\cap V_{pq}\neq\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\cap V_{pq}\neq\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p2.m41" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|S\cap V_{pq}|=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|S\cap V_{pq}|=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S3.p2.m42" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\cap V_{pq}=\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\cap V_{pq}=\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="S3.p2.m43" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v\in V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v\in V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>, and by structure of <math id="S3.p2.m44" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G,</annotation></semantics></math> we note that
<math id="S3.p2.m45" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v</annotation></semantics></math> is adjacent to every vertex in <math id="S3.p2.m46" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m47" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we obtain <math id="S3.p2.m48" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\cap V_{p}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\cap V_{p}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\cap V_{p^{2}}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\cap V_{p^{2}}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>. As there are no edges between <math id="S3.p2.m50" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>, so the set, <math id="S3.p2.m52" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S\setminus\{v\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S\setminus\{v\}</annotation></semantics></math> may be any subset of <math id="S3.p2.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus, any set in <math id="S3.p2.m54" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is of the form <math id="S3.p2.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\{v\}\cup S_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\{v\}\cup S_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p2.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v\in V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v\in V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m57" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle S_{q}\subseteq V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle S_{q}\subseteq V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>. With this information, the generating function for such sets is:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
