<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span>
Let <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n=pqr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n=pqr</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math> being prime numbers, and let <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G\cong\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G\cong\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})</annotation></semantics></math> be its zero divisor graph.The set of vertices <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V(G)</annotation></semantics></math> can be partitioned based on the greatest common divisor (gcd) with <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math>. For a divisor <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle n</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{d}=\{v\in V:\gcd(v,n)=d\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>gcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{d}=\{v\in V:\gcd(v,n)=d\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The possible values for <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d</annotation></semantics></math> for any non-zero zero divisor are <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p,q,r,pq,pr," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p,q,r,pq,pr,</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle qr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle qr</annotation></semantics></math>. The set of vertices is the disjoint union <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V=V_{p}\cup V_{q}\cup V_{r}\cup V_{pq}\cup V_{pr}\cup V_{qr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V=V_{p}\cup V_{q}\cup V_{r}\cup V_{pq}\cup V_{pr}\cup V_{qr}</annotation></semantics></math>. The size of each partition is given by Euler’s totient function <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle|V_{d}|=\varphi(\tfrac{n}{d})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle|V_{d}|=\varphi(\tfrac{n}{d})</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, we have</p>
