<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a\in V_{\rho_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a\in V_{\rho_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b\in V_{\rho_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b\in V_{\rho_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> be two distinct vertices. Their product <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle ab</annotation></semantics></math> is congruent to <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\pmod{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mspace style="width:0.949em;" width="0.949em"></mspace><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">mod</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\pmod{n}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if for each prime <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s\in\{p,q,r\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s\in\{p,q,r\}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s\mid ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s\mid ab</annotation></semantics></math>. An element <math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle v\in V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle v\in V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a multiple of <math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d</annotation></semantics></math> and is not divisible by any prime factor of <math id="S4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tfrac{n}{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>d</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tfrac{n}{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, <math id="S4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s\mid ab" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s\mid ab</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s\mid\rho_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s\mid\rho_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s\mid\rho_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s\mid\rho_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, <math id="S4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle b</annotation></semantics></math> are adjacent if and only if <math id="S4.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{lcm}(\rho_{1},\rho_{2})=pqr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>lcm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ρ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{lcm}(\rho_{1},\rho_{2})=pqr</annotation></semantics></math>. Also, each of <math id="S4.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> are independent set, since for any <math id="S4.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle a,b\in V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle a,b\in V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\operatorname{lcm}(d,d)=d\neq pqr" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>lcm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≠</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\operatorname{lcm}(d,d)=d\neq pqr</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle d\in\{p,q,r,pq,pr,qr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle d\in\{p,q,r,pq,pr,qr\}</annotation></semantics></math> and lcm is their least common multiple. The adjacency relations among <math id="S4.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{d}</annotation></semantics></math>’s are: <math id="S4.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> is completely connected to <math id="S4.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{qr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{qr}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{q}</annotation></semantics></math> is completely connected to <math id="S4.p2.m37" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pr}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> is completely connected to <math id="S4.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq}</annotation></semantics></math>. The sets <math id="S4.p2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq},V_{pr},V_{qr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq},V_{pr},V_{qr}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise completely connected, forming a complete tripartite subgraph, see Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of order 𝑝⁢𝑞⁢𝑟 ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
