<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>Partition structure of the zero divisor graph <math id="S4.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math> are primes.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we classify all independent sets in <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G.</annotation></semantics></math>
Let <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all independent sets of <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr})</annotation></semantics></math>. An independent set cannot contain vertices from two connected partitions. In particular, since <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle V_{pq},V_{pr},V_{qr}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle V_{pq},V_{pr},V_{qr}</annotation></semantics></math> are pairwise connected, any independent set can have a non-empty intersection with at most one of them. This allows us to partition <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{I}</annotation></semantics></math> into four disjoint families:</p>
