<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Eneström-Kakeya theorem (see, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>), states that: Given a real polynomial <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e(x)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}e_{i}x^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e(x)=\sum_{i=0}^{n}e_{i}x^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e_{i}\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e_{i}\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the zeros of <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle e(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle e(x)</annotation></semantics></math> lie in the annulus region: <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r\leq|x|\leq R," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r\leq|x|\leq R,</annotation></semantics></math>
where</p>
<table id="S4.Ex112" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex112.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\min\left\{\left|\frac{e_{i}}{e_{i+1}}\right|:0\leq i\leq n-1\right\}~\text{and}~R=\max\left\{\left|\frac{e_{i}}{e_{i+1}}\right|:0\leq i\leq n-1\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo rspace="0.278em">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo lspace="0.330em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mo>|</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mfrac><mo rspace="0.278em">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\min\left\{\left|\frac{e_{i}}{e_{i+1}}\right|:0\leq i\leq n-1\right\}~\text{and}~R=\max\left\{\left|\frac{e_{i}}{e_{i+1}}\right|:0\leq i\leq n-1\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p7.2" class="ltx_p">Next, we apply Eneström-Kakeya theorem to polynomial <math id="S4.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> and compare the region with Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 4.2. ‣ 4 Independence polynomial of zero divisor graph of order 𝑝⁢𝑞⁢𝑟 ‣ Combinatorial and analytic aspects of independence polynomials of zero divisor graphs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>. We have the following proposition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The maximum exponent in the polynomial <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(r-1)(p+q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(r-1)(p+q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and for <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> it is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(r-1)(p+q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(r-1)(p+q-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r-1&gt;(p-1)(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r-1&gt;(p-1)(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle pq+qr+pr-2(p+q+r)+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle pq+qr+pr-2(p+q+r)+3</annotation></semantics></math>, if <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r-1&lt;(p-1)(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r-1&lt;(p-1)(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r-1=(p-1)(q-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r-1=(p-1)(q-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, these two expressions are equal and maximal. The minimal exponent (degree) of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle(p-1)(q+r-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(p-1)(q+r-2)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proof.</span> Let <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I(\varGamma(\mathbb{Z}_{pqr}),x)</annotation></semantics></math> be the independence polynomial with primes be <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x=p-1,y=q-1,z=r-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x=p-1,y=q-1,z=r-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p,q,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p,q,r</annotation></semantics></math> are primes, <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p\geq 2,q\geq 3,r\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p\geq 2,q\geq 3,r\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, which implies <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x\geq 1,y\geq 2,z\geq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x\geq 1,y\geq 2,z\geq 4</annotation></semantics></math>. The condition <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle p&lt;q&lt;r</annotation></semantics></math> implies <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x&lt;y&lt;z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x&lt;y&lt;z</annotation></semantics></math>. In order to simplify calculations, we consider the following assignments</p>
